<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chuuk Lagoon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Truk Lagoon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuuk">Chuuk</a> Lagoon, formerly Truk, is a large lagoon enclosed by a coral reef in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>, now part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>. Under Japanese rule it became the main forward base of the Imperial Japanese Navy's Combined Fleet in the Pacific. US carrier aircraft devastated it in Operation Hailstone in February 1944, and the wrecks of dozens of sunken ships now draw divers.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chuuk-lagoon.jpg" alt="Chuuk Lagoon" width="320" height="248" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US Bureau of the Census · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chuuk_Lagoon_Municipalities_map.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chuuk Lagoon lies in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, about 1,800 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. A protective reef 225 kilometres around encloses a natural harbour 79 by 50 kilometres, with an area of 2,130 square kilometres. The atoll has eleven major islands and 46 smaller ones within the lagoon, plus 41 on the fringing reef. Chuuk means mountain in the Chuukese language, and the lagoon was known mainly as Truk, a mispronunciation of Ruk, until 1990.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence shows that the islands of Feefen and Wééné had human settlements in the first and second century BCE, and widespread settlement appeared in the 14th century CE. The Spanish navigator Álvaro de Saavedra made the first recorded European sighting in 1528. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed the islands but its control was nominal, and tribal warfare continued until the German colonial era after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> were sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in 1899. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> seized the German possessions in October 1914, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuuk">Chuuk</a> became Japanese under the South Seas Mandate.</p><p>In the Second World War Truk was the main forward anchorage of the Imperial Japanese Navy in the South Pacific, nicknamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> of the Pacific, though it was in fact lightly fortified. The Japanese built roads, bunkers, five airstrips, seaplane bases and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> station, and the battleships Yamato and Musashi were stationed there for months around 1943. The garrison numbered 27,856 naval and 16,737 army men.</p><p>Warned a week ahead, the Japanese withdrew their larger warships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a>. On 17 February 1944 American carrier aircraft began Operation Hailstone, which lasted three days. They sank 12 smaller warships and 32 merchant ships and destroyed 275 aircraft, mainly on the ground. British forces attacked again in June 1945 in Operation Inmate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chuuk Lagoon is part of Chuuk State in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>. Weno city, on Weno Island, is the capital of the atoll and of the state. Diving on the wrecks is the main industry, following Jacques Cousteau's 1971 television documentary. The Truk Lagoon Underwater Fleet was added to the US National Register of Historic Places in 1976, and the ships are classed as a Japanese war grave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chuuk Lagoon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan left the League in 1933 but kept the islands and turned <b>Truk</b> into a major naval base.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li></ul></section>
